# Task 9 report: fixtures and goldens (SMA-769)

## Implemented
- `make_fixtures.py`: a Responses builder (`RS`, ported from `support/streams.rs`). It writes
  `responses/conforming` (all 19 named fixtures plus `keepalive_events`, 4 golden sources),
  `responses/malformed` (36 fixtures, outcome Malformed, F4) and `responses/edge` (13 fixtures with
  their own outcome: upstream error, truncation, limits, record after terminal, SSE layer, clean).
- `fixtures.rs`: new functions only (F1): allow-all payload/`event:`/comment identity,
  canaries, I1, split points, malformed rows, edge rows, `a_large_lifecycle_event_passes`,
  `structured_output_keys_pass_on_the_stream`, `recorded_responses_streams_pass_unchanged`,
  `recorded_responses_cut_streams_are_rewritten`.
- Re-kept 11 recordings from the archive (run 1 of each pair). All 11 pass with no deviation, so the
  directory holds 28 `.sse` files. README updated (3 cut files; "not kept" text replaced).
- Goldens: 4 new files + manifest entries (`notice_positions`, position/id/namespace in `allowed`).
  Six old golden files and their manifest entries are byte-identical (diff is insertions only).
  I read each golden against the § 6.5 JSON: key order, repeated `sequence_number`, ids, notice text.

## Deviations and notes
- The brief said to add the Responses sets to the four existing chat tests. F1 allows only new
  functions, and observe mode leaks canaries on stream failures by design, so the Responses tests
  are separate functions.
- `truncated_item` is a conforming golden source (F5), but allow-all rewrites it (A3). Its row in
  the allow-all test asserts the rewrite instead of identity (`ALLOW_ALL_REWRITES`).
- Golden `truncated_item` is a cut call: openai-python raises on `response.incomplete` also on the
  upstream bytes, so the golden's client check excuses a raise only when the input raises too.
- Recorded split points: every point for files up to 12 KiB; for larger files (up to 185 KB) every
  point within 48 bytes of a record boundary and every 499th point. All points would cost O(n^2).
- Two `src` unit tests hard-coded the old file set (`17`, Function/Custom only, cut call at
  index 0): `responses/assembly.rs`, `responses/stream.rs`. I changed them to 28 files, any
  non-`Other` kind, and the cut call's real index.
- `edge/hold_limit` also drops an oversized terminal record (the full 20 KB args), as the Task 8
  generator does; the first failure is `hold_limit`.
- Secret check: no `authorization`, `bearer`, `sk-`, `org-`, `proj_`, `openai-organization` in any kept
  file (case-sensitive). A case-insensitive grep matches random base64 in `encrypted_content`.
  Only `.sse` was copied.

## Verification
- `cargo nextest run --locked -p paigasus-gateway-core --no-fail-fast`: 314 passed, 1 skipped.
- `cargo clippy --locked --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings && cargo fmt --check`: clean.
- `GEN` leaves `git status` with only the new fixture dirs; `UPDATE_GOLDEN=1` once.

## Fix round 1
- `responses_every_split_point_gives_the_same_output`: no fixture is skipped now. A fixture over
  6 KiB uses `split_points()` (hold_limit, oversized_*). The threshold is an exact check: the
  count of split-tested fixtures equals the count of fixtures in the three sets.
- `recorded_streams_pass_through_the_interceptor` (src/responses/stream.rs): the kind check is an
  allow-list: Function, Custom, Computer, Shell, ApplyPatch. No recording has the other kinds.
- Result: CORE 314 passed, 1 skipped. LINT clean.
